From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 20:18:50 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] msvcp140_atomic_wait: Take the time zone database from
 icu.dll, as Windows does.

std::chrono's get_tzdb(), current_zone() and every conversion through
a time_zone end in these exports, and Microsoft's library answers them
from icu.dll: the IANA zone names in order, with each link naming the
zone it stands for, ICU's data version, ICU's default zone, and for a
zone at an instant its offset, daylight saving amount, the transitions
around the instant and a short name.  Wine gave the Windows time zones'
display names instead, none of which a program asks for by name, and
__std_tzdb_get_sys_info and __std_tzdb_delete_sys_info were stubs, so
the first conversion aborted the process.  Word asks for the database
and the current zone at every start.

Now the same comes from icu.dll when there is one.  The byte after the
name passed to __std_tzdb_get_sys_info selects the offset alone or the
offset with its range, a zone with no transition on a side reaches to
DBL_MAX there, and a short name the file system code page cannot hold
becomes "+HH" or "+HHMM".  The leap seconds come from where Windows
keeps them, HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\LeapSecondInformation.
Without icu.dll the names are the Windows ones as before, and a
conversion fails with the error for a missing library instead of
aborting.  Run over Wine's icu.dll, tools/tzdbprobe prints the same
for these functions as Microsoft's msvcp140_atomic_wait.dll from
Office's installation does: 634 zones and 157 links, the zones'
offsets, ranges and short names, and the options.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

